from pathlib import Path
import html,json,hashlib,re,subprocess
TS='2026-09-06-07-57'; FN=f'Daily_Multivendor_Routing_Lab_{TS}.html'; Path('docs').mkdir(exist_ok=True); OUT=Path('docs')/FN

def fp(s): return hashlib.sha256(s.encode()).hexdigest()[:18]
# Each lesson: vendor, topic, source, class/date, foundation, purpose, mechanics, evidence, failure/lab
L=[
('Cisco Systems','OSPFv3 Prefix Suppression','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/c9000/lyr3-fwd/ospf/ospf-configuration-guide/prefix-suppression-support-for-ospfv3.html','Official Cisco OSPF Configuration Guide — updated July 17, 2026','OSPFv3 separates topology from most address information. Router- and Network-LSAs describe graph connectivity, while Intra-Area-Prefix-LSAs carry many attached IPv4/IPv6 prefixes. Prefix suppression can remove selected locally configured transit prefixes without removing the graph edge itself.','Reduce LSA scale, SPF work, flooding bandwidth and exposure of infrastructure-only addressing while retaining the topology needed to calculate paths.','A local interface participates in OSPFv3; the router originates topology state and separately decides whether its locally configured address prefix is eligible for suppression. Suppressed transit prefixes are omitted from prefix-bearing LSAs, while Router/Network-LSA connectivity remains. Remote routers can still compute a path through the link but no longer install a route to the hidden transit subnet.','Cisco documents global `prefix-suppression` under `router ospfv3` or an address family, plus interface-level suppression/disable overrides. `show ospfv3` asks whether the process has suppression enabled; `show ospfv3 interface` asks which interface overrides apply. The decisive proof is the remote LSDB/RIB: topology remains while the eligible transit prefix disappears.','Loopbacks, secondary addresses and passive-interface prefixes are documented exceptions to broad suppression. Do not confuse this with an arbitrary route filter. Lab: advertise a loopback plus transit /31, enable global suppression, compare remote LSDB/RIB, then use an interface override.'),
('Cisco Systems','EIGRP Wide Metrics and RIB Scaling','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios-xe/ip-routing/b-ip-routing/m_ire-wid-met.html','Official Cisco IOS XE IP Routing Guide — current 2026 publication','Classic EIGRP metric arithmetic loses useful resolution on modern high-speed interfaces. Wide Metrics use 64-bit calculations in named mode, with finer throughput and latency scaling and support for very high-speed links and bundles.','Preserve meaningful path differentiation on 10/40/100/400G and large EtherChannels where old formulas can make distinct paths look equivalent.','Interface bandwidth and delay feed the wide composite formula. Named EIGRP calculates the 64-bit metric, DUAL selects a successor/feasible alternatives, and a RIB scaling factor maps the larger internal value into the route table representation. The FIB then programs the selected path.','Verify named mode and K values before comparing metrics. Cisco documents `metric rib-scale`; changing it clears and reinstalls EIGRP routes in the RIB with newly scaled values. An Up neighbor proves adjacency, not that the metric formula has enough resolution to rank high-speed paths.','K-value mismatch can prevent adjacency; poor RIB scaling can collapse distinct EIGRP metrics after protocol selection. Lab: create two high-speed paths with small latency differences, compare named wide metrics and RIB-scaled values.'),
('Cisco Systems','BGP Route Flap Dampening Penalty and Reuse','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios-xe/ip-routing/b-ip-routing/m_irg-int-features-0.html','Official Cisco IOS XE BGP Configuration Guide — current 2026 publication','Dampening assigns an instability penalty to a route. Repeated withdrawal/re-advertisement raises the penalty. Crossing the suppress threshold hides the route; decay eventually lowers the penalty below the reuse threshold.','Contain a repeatedly unstable external route so it does not force endless UPDATE/withdraw propagation and downstream reconvergence.','A prefix flaps, receives penalty and enters history state. Penalties accumulate. When suppression threshold is crossed, BGP stops advertising/using the route even if it reappears. The penalty decays according to half-life. When it falls below reuse, the route becomes eligible again; max-suppress bounds how long it can stay hidden.','Cisco documents `bgp dampening ...`, `show ip bgp dampening flap-statistics`, and `show ip bgp dampening dampened-paths`. These commands answer whether the route is in history/damped state, its penalty and reuse time. A peer reset itself withdraws routes but does not add a dampening penalty.','Over-aggressive dampening can hide legitimate rapidly recovering destinations. Lab: flap one eBGP prefix repeatedly, observe history→damped→reuse transitions, then reset the peer and compare penalty behavior.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','PIM Source-Specific Multicast','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/ip-multicast/pim','Official Palo Alto Networks multicast documentation — current 2026','SSM is multicast where the receiver requests a specific source and group, (S,G). Unlike Any-Source Multicast, true SSM does not need an RP for source discovery. Receiver signaling normally uses IGMPv3 so the source address can be named.','Remove RP/source-discovery complexity for applications that know the sender and want only that sender’s traffic.','The host sends an IGMPv3 source-specific membership report. The receiver-facing firewall creates (S,G) state and sends a PIM Join toward the source according to unicast RPF. Intermediate routers install (S,G) state and the source stream follows the source tree.','Verify PIM on receiver-facing interfaces, IGMPv3 membership, the SSM group range and the unicast route used for RPF toward S. A healthy RP mapping is not evidence for SSM because RP discovery is not in the path.','IGMPv2/group-only signaling or a group outside the SSM range can defeat the intended model. Lab: use a 232/8 group with IGMPv3, then change group/version and compare (S,G) state.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','BGP Route Dampening Profiles','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/advanced-routing/create-bgp-routing-profiles','Official Palo Alto Networks Advanced Routing documentation — current 2026','PAN-OS Advanced Routing exposes suppress/reuse limits, maximum hold time, and separate reachable/unreachable decay half-lives in a BGP Dampening Profile.','Limit churn from unstable prefixes without tearing down the entire neighbor session.','Each flap contributes to instability state. The profile determines when accumulated penalty suppresses routes and how quickly reachable/unreachable state decays. When the penalty drops below reuse, the route can participate again.','Create the Dampening Profile and then verify that the intended peer or peer group actually references it. A perfect profile object with no attachment changes nothing. Inspect suppression/reuse/decay fields and peer inheritance/override.','Do not treat dampening as a BGP hold timer or BFD function. Lab: apply two profiles with different thresholds to test peers and compare how quickly identical flap patterns become suppressed/reused.'),
('Palo Alto Networks','OSPF Area Range Summarization and Suppression','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/ospf/configure-ospf','Official Palo Alto Networks OSPF documentation — current 2026','An OSPF ABR summarizes intra-area routes into inter-area reachability. PAN-OS Area Range can advertise an aggregate or suppress matching inter-area advertisement by disabling Advertise.','Reduce inter-area LSA scale and hide internal addressing details at the area boundary.','Specific routes exist in the source area. The ABR matches them against the configured Area Range. With Advertise enabled it originates a summary into other areas; with Advertise disabled it suppresses the matching inter-area advertisement. Remote routers calculate routes from the ABR-generated state.','Inspect the exact range/mask, Advertise setting and resulting LSDB on another area. Area Range is not the same as external-route summarization, which is a separate OSPF control.','A summary can attract traffic for a component that is not truly reachable if design assumptions are wrong. Lab: summarize four /24s into /22, withdraw one component, and inspect remote forwarding behavior.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Route Tags Driving SD-WAN Rules','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.2.7/administration-guide/672387/using-bgp-tags-with-sd-wan-rules','Official Fortinet Administration Guide — FortiOS 7.2.7','FortiGate can translate BGP attributes such as communities into an internal route tag. SD-WAN rules can match route tags as dynamic destinations, avoiding hard-coded changing prefixes.','Steer dynamically learned site/application prefixes over chosen WAN members while preserving dynamic routing.','A BGP UPDATE arrives with a community or other match condition. An inbound route map applies `set-route-tag`. The tagged route is installed. A new session destination matches the tagged route and therefore the SD-WAN service; health/strategy then chooses an eligible member.','Verify the neighbor route-map attachment, the learned route tag, the SD-WAN rule tag and member/SLA state. A BGP community by itself is not the same thing as the FortiGate route tag consumed by SD-WAN.','Route classification and member selection are separate. Lab: advertise two route sets with different communities, map them to different tags, steer each through a different WAN member and then fail an SLA.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Neighbor Groups with Multiple Remote AS Values','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/8.0.0/administration-guide/884937/assigning-multiple-remote-autonomous-systems-to-a-single-bgp-neighbor-group','Official Fortinet Administration Guide — FortiOS 8.0.0','`remote-as-filter` lets one neighbor group admit peers from a controlled set of ASNs using an AS-path list rather than a single fixed `remote-as`.','Scale hub/spoke peering when spokes legitimately use different ASNs without duplicating nearly identical neighbor groups.','A connection matches the dynamic neighbor range/group. BGP OPEN presents the remote ASN. FortiGate evaluates that ASN against the configured filter before normal route exchange. Permitted peers proceed to import/export policy and route processing.','Fortinet documents that `remote-as` and `remote-as-filter` are mutually exclusive. Verify the AS-path list, neighbor group, neighbor-range and resulting peer role before investigating route policy.','Leaving a fixed remote-as while attempting to use remote-as-filter creates the wrong configuration model. Lab: permit two spoke ASNs, reject a third, then demonstrate the mutual-exclusion rule.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Community Lists and Route-Map Matching','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/8.0.0/administration-guide/597174/community-lists','Official Fortinet Administration Guide — FortiOS 8.0.0','A community-list defines BGP community values or regular-expression matches. A route-map references that list with `match-community` so policy can act on the route.','Apply routing intent to many prefixes using metadata instead of individual prefix enumeration.','An UPDATE carries communities. BGP stores attributes. A community-list evaluates the values; the route-map rule references the list and, if matched, permits/denies or changes route attributes/tags. Accepted routes then continue through BGP best-path/RIB logic.','Use `config router community-list` to define the match and `set match-community` inside the route-map rule. Verify the actual route community and numeric rule ordering. An unused community-list has no forwarding effect.','Expanded regexes can match more than intended. Lab: tag three routes differently, use standard and expanded lists, and verify only intended routes receive the action.'),
('Arista Networks','Segment Routing Traffic Engineering Policy','https://www.arista.com/en/support/toi/tag/segment-routing','Arista engineering TOI — SR-TE material updated July 16, 2026','An SR Policy is a headend steering instruction containing a segment list. Transit routers execute Node/Adjacency SID forwarding and do not maintain RSVP-style per-policy tunnel state.','Engineer explicit low-latency, maintenance or constraint-aware paths with a simple segment-routing core.','IGP distributes SR SIDs. The headend learns/configures candidate paths and activates one according to policy/preference. A route/steering rule binds traffic to the SR Policy. Ingress pushes the SID stack; each transit LSR executes the top label and the egress performs the final lookup.','Verify active candidate path, segment list, imposed label stack and LFIB. A complete SR IGP database only proves SID reachability; it does not prove packets are steered into the intended policy.','A reachable but wrong segment list can silently take the wrong path. Lab: configure two candidate lists with different preferences, switch preference, and capture the ingress stack.'),
('Arista Networks','MLAG Reload Delay','https://www.arista.com/assets/data/docs/Manuals/EOS-4.15.0F-Manual.pdf','Official Arista EOS User Manual — command semantics retained in current families','After reboot, an MLAG peer needs time to rebuild control-plane and hardware forwarding state. Reload delay intentionally holds MLAG/non-MLAG links down during that window.','Prevent a rebooted peer from attracting traffic before VLAN, MAC, routing and forwarding tables are ready.','Peer reboots, initializes MLAG/forwarding, and recovers peer-link/control state. The reload-delay timer keeps selected front-panel links disabled while state is rebuilt. When the timer expires, links come up and the topology can reconverge onto the peer.','`reload-delay mlag <seconds>` controls the hold. Verify peer-link state, MLAG state and remaining delay before treating disabled links as a fault. Platform readiness can justify different values.','Too short risks blackholing; too long extends recovery. Lab: compare a short test value with a safer value during a peer reboot while sending traffic.'),
('Arista Networks','IGMP Snooping Querier','https://www.arista.com/en/assets/data/pdf/user-manual/um-books/EOS-User-Manual.pdf','Official Arista EOS User Manual — 2026 publication','IGMP snooping learns interested ports from membership reports. In a pure Layer-2 VLAN with no multicast router sending queries, memberships can age out. A snooping querier sends membership queries so the switch can maintain the table.','Avoid unknown multicast flooding and membership loss in L2-only multicast VLANs.','The querier sends a query with a configured source address. Hosts answer with reports. EOS updates per-VLAN group/port state. Multicast frames then replicate only toward interested ports/router ports.','Global `ip igmp snooping querier` is overridden by per-VLAN controls; EOS also needs a usable querier source address and VLAN/SVI conditions. Version 2 is the documented default.','Enabling the switch globally without a source address can still leave no useful queries. Lab: let memberships age without a router, then enable querier with source address and verify persistence.')